cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Governance Canon: CCTF, CSRF, and Triple Governance"
message: "If you reference these frameworks, please cite this archive."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Tillar
    given-names: James Andrew
    affiliation: Tillar Technologies
abstract: >-
  Three frameworks for building and evaluating governed intelligent systems, archived with
  their concept-level provenance. CCTF (Contextual Compatibility and Transformation Framework)
  asks what an intelligent system should represent and how it should act under partial
  observation. CSRF (Constitutional Scientific Reasoning Framework) asks what procedures make
  claims reliable regardless of what kind of system makes them. Triple Governance specifies
  what an idea is permitted to do, as distinct from where it came from. CCTF and CSRF were
  split from a single unified program specifically so that neither is permitted to validate the other; the
  only sanctioned interface is a preregistered 2×2 factorial. All status lines, mundane nulls,
  and falsifiers are reproduced unmodified; one operational section is redacted and the
  redaction is declared. No comparative experiment has been run.
keywords:
  - AI governance
  - scientific reasoning
  - provenance
  - falsifiability
  - auditability
  - governed agents
